# nest-scaffold

This project's modules, services, controllers{{#if isMicroservice}}, and RPC
methods{{else}}, and routes{{/if}} are generated with the `nest-scaffold`
CLI — do not hand-write new ones. Hand-writing a new
module/controller/service, or adding a method by editing the
controller/service directly, produces a shape that doesn't match the rest of
the codebase (missing `AppModule` registration{{#if isMicroservice}}, missing
`.proto` sync{{/if}}, inconsistent error-catalog wiring, etc.).

## When to use this skill

- Any request for a new feature/resource/module, even named informally
  ("add invoices", "we need a way to track X")
- Any request for a new {{#if isMicroservice}}RPC/{{/if}}endpoint on an
  existing feature, even phrased as a capability ("let users do X", "admins
  should be able to Y")
- The user explicitly says "generate", "scaffold", or names the CLI

## When NOT to use this skill

- Filling in business logic inside a method the CLI already generated
- Editing entity fields, DTO validation, or existing method bodies
- Bug fixes and refactors
- The project has no `nest-scaffold.config.json` at its root

## How to use it

Read `.agents/skills/nest-scaffold.md` for full command syntax, flags, and
worked examples for `generate module/service/controller/method` before
running anything — do not guess flags.

Quick shape, always run from the project root:

```bash
npx nest-scaffold generate module <name> --full
npx nest-scaffold generate method <module> <methodName> --type <get|post|put|patch|delete>
```
{{#if hasAuth}}
This project has JWT auth, but `generate` leaves new endpoints **public** — add
`@UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)` yourself to protect them (see the auth note in
`.agents/skills/nest-scaffold.md`).
{{/if}}
